' bug-487 repro: an operand that MUTATES a resource's STATE, inside an
' expression whose other operand points INTO that same STATE block.
'
' `collections::append(f.state.xs, sideEffect(f))`
'    arg 0 -> a pointer into f's STATE block
'    arg 1 -> a call that appends to f.state.xs, growing and REALLOCATING that
'             same block, which frees the allocation arg 0 points into
'
' arg 0 then dangles, and `append` reads a freed block. Symptom depends only on
' what lands in the freed memory: `Allocation failed` (7-701-0001) when the
' garbage is read as a capacity, SIGSEGV when it is read as a pointer.
'
' Per `mfb spec language resource-management` §15 this program is well-formed:
' a `RES` is an alias to one live resource, and "a state update made through a
' RES parameter (an alias, not a copy) is visible to the owner after the call".
' Nothing forbids making that update from inside an operand.
'
' NOTE the expected result is NOT 2 elements per round. Under value semantics
' arg 0 is a snapshot taken before `sideEffect` runs, so the outer append
' overwrites the nested one and the defined answer is `len == rounds`, all 222.
' Losing the nested append is fine. CRASHING is the bug.
IMPORT collections
IMPORT io
IMPORT fs

TYPE St
  xs AS List OF Integer
  n  AS Integer
END TYPE

FUNC sideEffect(RES f AS fs::File STATE St) AS Integer
  f.state.xs = collections::append(f.state.xs, 111)
  f.state.n = f.state.n + 1
  RETURN 222
END FUNC

FUNC main AS Integer
  RES f AS fs::File STATE St = fs::openFile("project.json")
  f.state.n = 0
  MUT i AS Integer = 0
  WHILE i < 3
    f.state.xs = collections::append(f.state.xs, sideEffect(f))
    i = i + 1
  END WHILE
  io::print("len=" & toString(len(f.state.xs)))
  io::print("n=" & toString(f.state.n))
  fs::close(f)
  RETURN 0
END FUNC
